I just finished a game with a chaotic priest where my main
weapons for much of the game were spears. There were a
couple of things I learned about spears that were counter
intuitive.

First, based on my flawed memory of D&D, priests always used
bashing things instead of cutting things. Granted you don't
cut things with a spear, but poking a hole in something so
it will leak and die is a lot more like cutting than
bashing. I was surprised to find a priest could become
skilled with spears.

Somewhere after the castle I wanted to enchant my +6 silver
spear up to +7. To my dismay, spears write in the dust like
a club instead of engraving, so I couldn't dull my spear
back to +5 that way. Now a spear is a lot more like a
writing instrument than a sword. It's long and skinny with a
pointy end! Harumph!

Early on, when I realized I had screwed up and would most
likely never see Mojo, I was reading the spoilers to pick
weapons. I saw there was a dwarven spear, top of the line. I
couldn't ever remember seeing one. Well, I found three in
this game but the silver spear was special and the advantage
of enchanting many normal spears at once made it impractical
to enchant the dwarven spears.

Maybe someday I'll play a game where I'll actually find a
magic marker. I have just finished two, 70,000+ turn games
without ever finding a magic marker.

